Spec: specs/002-capture-and-canonical-events/spec.md (Parser seam; Anthropic parser: Parses, Decoding, Events, Hashed payload, Tolerance; AC22, AC45, AC46, AC47)
Research: specs/002-capture-and-canonical-events/research.md Q2 (block type names), Q4 (HEAD /api/hello skipped), Q5 (tool_call_id from tool_use_id), Q6 (reasoning_requested rule), Q7 (DecodeLimit as the decode cap), Q16 (event half of AC22)
Why this approach: the request is read into a struct of json.RawMessage fields, so unknown fields stay in the raw body and system, tools and blocks reach Canonicalize as sent; cache_hints walks the decoded tree for the key at any depth. Each block is read through a small struct; a block that can't be read or has an unmapped type is unknown with its JSON kept. Tool events follow their message in block order, which is what Canonicalize's tool-block lookup expects. Both bodies go through contentcoding.Decode, and the status is the worst of the two. Details recorded in plan.md ("Details fixed in T20").
Alternatives rejected: keeping a string message content as a raw JSON string block (it would never dedup with the same text sent as a block); failing the parse on a wrong-typed block field (the spec's tolerance keeps unknown content); a tolerant partial pass over a truncated request body (T21 builds that for responses; a request over 32 MiB is left as partial with no request events).
Trade-off / known limit: the response body is decoded but not parsed until T21/T22, so a messages exchange is ok with request-side events only. A truncated request yields no events. reasoning_requested is true for a non-object thinking, by the letter of Q6's rule.
